<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean-Jacques Rousseau</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1712–1778</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Rousseau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean-Jacques Rousseau was a Genevan-born philosopher, writer and composer whose works shaped the political thought of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>. In The Social Contract (1762) he argued that legitimate government rests on the general will of the people, and in Emile he set out a theory of education. His Confessions and novel Julie influenced the Romantic movement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau.jpg" alt="Jean-Jacques Rousseau" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maurice Quentin de La Tour (Life time: 1788) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maurice_Quentin_de_La_Tour_-_Portrait_of_Jean-Jacques_Rousseau_-_adjusted.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
